{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And they called to the people.</b> It was necessary to mention ״the people,״ and it would not have sufficed to say ״and they called to them.״ It intends to say that they called to those who had not gone out to wander from their tents — they themselves went to them and called them. Or it may mean that besides the fact that they went one by one, they additionally called them to go in groups to the sacrifices of their gods, etc." "<b>To the sacrifices of their gods.</b> And although it says (Sanhedrin 64a) that Peor's mode of worship involves no slaughter but rather the loosening of the bowels before it, and according to the plain sense they did not slaughter to it — perhaps they would make a feast of meat in its honor, or perhaps they would do so in order to increase the foods so as to expose themselves before it through the profuse discharge of filth." "<b>And they bowed, etc., and attached themselves.</b> That is, this is the reason for their bowing — that they attached themselves, etc. This is as they said, of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 64a), that Baal Peor misleads its worshippers."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {1 "הַתְרָזַת הַלִּכְלוּךְ and הַטִּנּוּף rendered literally per the translator's instruction to leave the Talmudic idiom (bared excretion before the idol of Peor) carrying exactly what he wrote."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:14:11.021726810Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}